            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Years and Education</strong><br>\nMikhail Kalashnikov was born in 1919 in the village of Kurya, Russia. His family was composed of peasants, and he received basic education before he was called to serve in the Soviet Army in 1938. His mechanical skills were evident early on, and he would soon use them to create one of the most recognized weapons in the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Entry into the Soviet Army</strong><br>\nDuring his service in the Soviet Red Army, Kalashnikov's engineering talent became apparent. He began inventing several modifications for Soviet tanks during World War II. His experiences in the war, particularly the drawbacks of contemporary weaponry, led him to design a new rifle.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Invention of the AK-47</strong><br>\nAfter being wounded in battle in 1941, Kalashnikov started working on various weapon designs. By 1947, he had completed the design for the Avtomat Kalashnikova model 1947, better known as the AK-47. It was officially adopted by the Soviet military in 1949.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The AK-47's Impact on Warfare</strong><br>\nThe AK-47 quickly became famous for its durability, ease of use, and reliability under harsh conditions. It became the standard issue rifle for Soviet and Warsaw Pact armies and has been used in conflicts around the world, significantly impacting modern warfare.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Recognition and Awards</strong><br>\nKalashnikov received numerous awards for his work, including the Stalin Prize for the AK-47. He was celebrated as a hero of the Soviet Union and later Russia for his contributions to the Soviet military's strength.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Continued Work and Improvements</strong><br>\nEven after the success of the AK-47, Kalashnikov continued to improve his design and work on other weapons. He stayed active in the development of small arms for the Soviet and then Russian army for many years.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
